const blogSeeds = [
  ['The course brochure is not the course', 'Admissions', 'Students', 'Before comparing college names, compare the compulsory work you will repeat for three or four years.', 'A brochure shows possibilities. A timetable shows reality. Ask for the current syllabus, assessment pattern, lab access and a sample first-year schedule before deciding.\n\nA student who loves the title but avoids the compulsory work is not choosing a dream; they are choosing a conflict. Keep one alternative open until the daily work has been tested.'],
  ['What “good placement” should actually mean', 'Employment', 'Families', 'Placement percentages hide role quality, eligibility filters and students who quietly opt out.', 'Ask which students were eligible, which roles were offered, whether offers were unique students or total offers, and how many joined. Then inspect location, shifts, bond terms and the actual work.\n\nThe useful question is not “Did a famous company visit?” It is “Could my student build evidence for a role they would accept?”'],
  ['A NO-NO list is not negativity', 'Self-discovery', 'Students', 'Eliminating work conditions that reliably drain you can create more honest options than naming a dream career.', 'Many teenagers cannot describe work they love because they have barely encountered work. They can often identify what they do not want: clinical exposure, unstable income, constant travel or desk-only days.\n\nTreat each NO-NO as a hypothesis. Test it safely, record what happened, and distinguish discomfort from a true boundary.'],
  ['Parents: support without silently steering', 'Family', 'Parents', 'A practical family constraint belongs in the decision, but it should be named instead of disguised as the student’s preference.', 'Say the constraint plainly: budget, care responsibility, safety, distance or uncertainty. Then invite the student to propose routes that respect it and one route that tests whether it can change.\n\nShared evidence reduces hidden conflict. The student should still own the final reflection and be able to explain the trade-off.'],
  ['Why one serious college project matters', 'Projects', 'College', 'A defensible project can connect special interest, faculty mentorship, internships and campus interviews.', 'Choose a real problem with accessible users, data or materials. Define a six-week first version and decide what evidence will show progress. A fashionable topic without access or ownership usually produces a weak story.\n\nDocument decisions, failed approaches and what you personally did. Interviewers often learn more from the reasoning than from the polished final screen.'],
  ['Top ten is useful evidence—not an identity', 'Performance', 'Students', 'Strong rank can keep selective doors open, but fear-driven rank chasing can erase curiosity and recovery.', 'Track rank only where institutions or recruiters actually use it. Pair it with subject-level understanding, projects, communication and evidence of recovery after setbacks.\n\nA rank is a dated comparison inside one cohort. It cannot measure judgment, kindness, stamina, originality or the conditions under which someone learns best.'],
  ['The first job is a launchpad, not a verdict', 'Employment', 'Graduates', 'Your first offer may solve money and experience before it solves meaning.', 'Evaluate the first role by its learning density, manager access, work standards, financial runway and ability to create credible outcomes. Review fit every six to twelve months.\n\nA sensible first job can fund a deliberate move. Keep a record of outcomes, protect confidential information and build one capability that compounds across employers.'],
  ['How to speak with a working professional', 'Research', 'Students', 'Do not ask only whether a career is good. Ask what an ordinary Tuesday looks like.', 'Request twenty minutes and arrive with specific questions: recurring tasks, difficult trade-offs, early mistakes, changing tools, and what newcomers misunderstand. Do not ask for a job in the first conversation.\n\nAfterward, write one surprise, one concern and one low-risk experiment. First-person experience is evidence, not a universal rule.'],
  ['When a famous college is the wrong college', 'College choice', 'Families', 'Prestige cannot compensate for the wrong course, unaffordable debt or a daily environment that prevents the student from learning.', 'Compare department-level teaching, compulsory curriculum, commute, language, accessibility, cost, project supervision and the roles graduates actually enter.\n\nUse rankings to locate questions, not end the conversation. A less famous institution with strong fit and lower disruption can produce better evidence and wellbeing.'],
  ['AI can explain; it cannot own the decision', 'AI', 'Everyone', 'Students need verification habits and preserved deep skills, not a simple ban or unlimited automation.', 'Record what AI claimed, which current source was checked, what changed, and who remains accountable. Never paste sensitive school, health, identity or family information into an unapproved tool.\n\nProtect weekly unaided practice in writing, calculation, observation and explanation. The goal is augmented judgment—not invisible learning debt.'],
  ['Choosing Grade 11 subjects without panic', 'School', 'Grade 10', 'Start with available combinations, compulsory reading, workload and adjacent routes—not inherited prestige.', 'List the combinations actually offered within a realistic commute. Remove those with firm, tested NO-NOs, then sample the remaining subjects through real tasks.\n\nKeep two routes visible until school availability, foundational readiness and future eligibility are checked on current official sources.'],
  ['The hidden cost of a long qualification path', 'Finance', 'Families', 'Time, attempts, relocation and delayed earnings deserve the same attention as tuition fees.', 'Build a conservative cost map: fees, housing, travel, tools, exam attempts and the income that may be delayed. Add a recovery plan if the primary gate does not open.\n\nA long path can be worthwhile when the daily work and regulated responsibility fit. It should not be selected only because the title is respected.'],
  ['What to do when Grade 11 goes badly', 'Recovery', 'Students', 'A difficult transition is information—not proof that the entire stream was a mistake.', 'Separate missing foundations, study rhythm, teaching access, health, language and genuine subject conflict. Repair one bottleneck for four weeks and measure the change.\n\nIf the daily work remains a strong NO-NO after support and honest practice, investigate bridge routes early. Recovery and redirection are both legitimate outcomes.'],
  ['Portfolio evidence for non-design students', 'Projects', 'College', 'Every field can show reasoning, improvement and contribution without turning life into personal branding.', 'A useful portfolio might include a lab investigation, audit, policy memo, teaching resource, financial model, field diary or process improvement. Explain the context, your role, constraints and result.\n\nRemove confidential data and inflated claims. A reviewer should be able to understand what you learned and ask you a serious follow-up question.'],
  ['Campus interview preparation starts before final year', 'Employment', 'College', 'The final year should combine project evidence, eligibility protection, role research and repeated practice.', 'In second and third year, learn which roles visit campus and what their selection rounds test. Choose projects and internships that create relevant stories rather than collecting unrelated certificates.\n\nBy final year, practise explaining fundamentals and decisions aloud. Maintain an off-campus and higher-study alternative without diluting the primary plan.'],
  ['Family legacy: asset, obligation, or both?', 'Family', 'Families', 'A family profession or business can offer trust and access while also narrowing imagination.', 'Name what is genuinely available: mentoring, customers, capital, reputation or a proven apprenticeship. Then name the obligation: expected location, hierarchy, risk or lack of exit.\n\nTest one role inside the legacy and one outside alternative on equal evidence. Inheritance should become a conscious option, not an unnamed command.'],
  ['How much uncertainty can you actually carry?', 'Self-discovery', 'Everyone', 'Risk tolerance changes with savings, health, dependants and career stage.', 'Use a one-to-ten scale only as a conversation starter. Ask what kind of uncertainty is hardest: income, location, identity, schedule or social judgment.\n\nThen design guardrails—a savings runway, adjacent credential, deadline or fallback role. Courage without a recovery plan can become avoidable pressure.'],
  ['A realistic way to test medicine', 'Career reality', 'Students', 'Interest in biology is not the same as tolerance for clinical responsibility.', 'Explore the ordinary work: long training, illness, bodily exposure, night duties, documentation, uncertainty and patient communication. Use age-appropriate material and conversations rather than graphic shock tests.\n\nIf clinical exposure is a firm boundary, investigate health careers with different daily realities. Verify every eligibility claim officially.'],
  ['Why “follow your passion” is incomplete advice', 'Self-discovery', 'Students', 'Passion without access, practice or trade-offs can create guilt instead of direction.', 'Look for repeated attention: problems you return to after difficulty, people you want to help, and skills you are willing to practise. Add constraints such as income, family time and location openly.\n\nA calling can guide experiments. It does not excuse ignoring money, duty or evidence.'],
  ['How to compare an internship offer', 'Experience', 'College', 'A recognisable logo is less important than supervision, work quality and evidence you are allowed to retain.', 'Ask who will supervise you, what an ordinary week contains, whether the role is paid, what expenses you carry, and how performance is reviewed. Clarify confidentiality and ownership.\n\nPrefer an experience where you can make, observe, improve or support something real—and explain the learning afterward.'],
  ['The problem with collecting certificates', 'Learning', 'Students', 'Completion badges can crowd out the difficult practice that produces capability.', 'Before starting a course, name the task it should help you perform and the artifact or assessment that will prove it. Set a stopping rule if it duplicates what you already know.\n\nOne course followed by a credible project and feedback often creates more evidence than ten disconnected completions.'],
  ['Location is a career variable', 'Location', 'Families', 'Commute, language, industry access, care responsibilities and safety change what a route costs.', 'Early school recommendations should be strongly location-aware. College can widen the radius when the educational benefit justifies disruption. Employment may widen it again—but not for everyone.\n\nRecord the current mobility boundary and its reason. Revisit it at each major transition rather than treating it as permanent.'],
  ['What a healthy parent-student review sounds like', 'Family', 'Parents', 'Replace “Have you decided?” with questions about evidence and the next experiment.', 'Ask what was ruled out, what changed their mind, which source is current, and what small test would reduce uncertainty. Share financial or family constraints without presenting them as moral failure.\n\nEnd with one agreed action and a review date. Constant monitoring can make honest uncertainty harder to share.'],
  ['Preparing for a role AI is changing', 'AI', 'College', 'Track tasks being assisted before making dramatic predictions about whole careers.', 'Read recent job descriptions and speak with practitioners. Sort tasks into AI assists, human leads, human accountable and likely changing. Add dates because tool adoption and policies move quickly.\n\nBuild domain depth, verification, collaboration and ethical escalation—not only tool familiarity.'],
  ['The quiet strength of an adjacent route', 'Pathways', 'Students', 'A diploma, apprenticeship or related degree can preserve the underlying goal when the primary gate closes.', 'Define the goal beneath the title: the problems, setting, people or contribution. Then compare routes by access, time, cost, bridge options and actual work.\n\nAn alternative should be credible enough to choose, not a punishment invented after results arrive.'],
  ['How to review a college placement report', 'Research', 'Families', 'Treat every percentage as a claim that needs a denominator and definition.', 'Check the reporting year, programme, eligible cohort, unique students placed, multiple offers, median compensation, role families and students pursuing other routes.\n\nAsk current students where the report feels accurate and where it hides variation. Never convert an institution-wide number into an individual guarantee.'],
  ['Dream work may require several moves', 'Employment', 'Professionals', 'Six to twelve years can be a mobility window, not a deadline for becoming impressive.', 'Define dream work by daily problems, autonomy, people, environment and impact. Review capability gaps at years one, three, six, nine and twelve.\n\nSome moves will optimise learning or stability rather than title. Reaching meaningful work late is not failure, and sustaining it may require redefining success.'],
  ['When staying put is a thoughtful choice', 'Employment', 'Professionals', 'Not everyone wants continuous promotion, larger teams or public status.', 'An individual contributor may value craft, predictability, family time or deep focus. Evaluate whether learning, compensation, respect and health remain acceptable.\n\nDeclining promotion can be an active choice. Keep external evidence and relationships current so comfort does not quietly become lack of mobility.'],
  ['A better question than “What are you interested in?”', 'Self-discovery', 'Counsellors', 'Ask what the student avoids, repeats, notices and is willing to practise.', 'Broad interest words produce broad careers. Concrete prompts reveal more: Which reading do you postpone? Which difficult task still holds attention? What environment drains you? Whose problem do you notice?\n\nUse answers to design experiments, not assign a personality label. Context and preferences can change.'],
  ['Our editorial promise', 'Team', 'Everyone', 'We will distinguish evidence, experience, interpretation and recommendation.', 'Career guidance can easily sound certain while hiding weak assumptions. We will date changing claims, link primary sources where available, disclose limits and invite challenge.\n\nWe will not rank a student, sell a deterministic destiny or treat community popularity as proof. The final decision remains with the person who must live it.'],
];

